I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then use the installed indexing/search and GSAS-II tooling to identify candidate phases, refine them jointly, and export the requested project and artifacts. I’ll keep phase count conservative and report any ambiguity explicitly.

The chemistry strongly suggests manganese tungstate formation, but the unknown phase count means I won’t assume a single product. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the local crystallographic search/indexing interfaces before refinement.

The principal peak set is consistent with wolframite-type MnWO₄. Several strong reflections do not belong to that phase, so I’m testing plausible residual reactants and the alternative ternary Mn₃WO₆ against the complete peak list rather than assigning them from one line.

A single wolframite MnWO₄ model accounts for all prominent lines across 10–100° 2θ; Mn₃WO₆, Mn₃O₄, and common WO₃ polymorphs predict strong low-/mid-angle peaks that are absent. I’m refining the MnWO₄ cell, profile, background, and symmetry-allowed coordinates now, then I’ll inspect the difference curve for any defensible minor phase.

The first refinement confirms the phase assignment but exposes a profile-model issue: with no calibrated instrument function, unconstrained peak-shape terms become nonphysical and the formal counting-statistical Rwp is inflated. I’m tightening the model to physically meaningful broadening terms and removing a redundant scale parameter before treating the fit as final.

Allowing a constant 2θ offset improves the profile substantially (Rwp ≈ 8.7%), but it is strongly entangled with the unit-cell scale. Because the experiment is in reflection geometry, I’m testing the physically appropriate specimen-height shift instead; this should give a more defensible cell while preserving the phase/fraction conclusion.

The specimen-height model gives essentially the same fit while yielding a chemically/literature-consistent wolframite cell. I’ve also constrained the weakly determined oxygen positions to the reference structure; unconstrained oxygen refinement improved Rwp cosmetically but produced implausible W–O distances, so it is not defensible. The final model will cover the entire measured range and document that limitation.

The final constrained model is complete. I’m doing a last consistency check now: CIF parsability/stoichiometry, project phase and refinement flags, full-profile row count and arithmetic, and JSON paths/statistics against the saved GSAS-II project.

Determined one crystalline phase: wolframite MnWO₄, space group P2/c, with normalized crystalline weight fraction 1.000.

Fit: Rwp 9.592%, Rp 7.083%, GOF 2.772. Trace impurities or amorphous material cannot be quantified reliably.

